From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xun2202 <98207886+Xun2202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 09:12:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] downloads: finish videos with fewer SAF copies and show why a
 download failed

On HarmonyOS ZhuoyiTong the download folder's DocumentsProvider does not
support renameDocument, so every rename in the download pipeline silently
became a full copy of the video: cache -> "<ep>.tmp", "<ep>.tmp" ->
"<ep>.mkv.anikku" and finally "<ep>_tmp/" -> "<ep>/". A finished video was
written three times through SAF after ffmpeg reported 100%, and any failure
in that stretch left the episode with an exclamation mark, no notification
that is visible in the container, and no log line (one path set ERROR
without reporting at all; ffmpeg failures said only "Error in ffmpeg!").

- copyIntoDownloadDir writes the final name directly while an empty
  "<ep>.tmp" marker exists and deletes the marker after verifying the size;
  getOrDownloadVideoFile treats a leftover marker as "interrupted" and
  discards the video next to it (also covers the old partial ".tmp" file).
- renameToOrCopy(targetName, resolver): directory fallback first tries
  DocumentsContract.moveDocument for each child (no data through the app),
  merges into an existing target directory instead of failing, verifies
  copied sizes, and keeps the copy when only the source delete fails.
- The final directory rename is retried (2 s, 4 s) and the muxed video
  stays in the private cache behind a ".muxed" marker when the copy into
  the download folder fails, so retrying does not download again.
- Download.error / Download.fail(): every ERROR path records a reason; the
  download queue row shows it under the episode name ("Error: ..."),
  including the last ffmpeg warnings, the unexpected directory contents, or
  "interrupted" when the job was stopped.
- A failed ffprobe no longer aborts the download with an empty message; the
  duration is only used for the progress percentage.

Harmony-only: Mihon has the same renameToOrCopy fallback for chapter
directories (same framework, smaller files) and should get the move/merge
part; Animeko has its own downloader.
